00:00:07.909 --> 00:00:10.150 align:start position:0%
for the past three weeks  hundreds of indigenous protesters have  been camping in the heart of the capital  brasilia  they've come from all over brazil even  the most remote villages of the amazon  the plan was to unite more than 40  indigenous peoples  and to stop congress from approving a  controversial bill  backed by the powerful farm lobby and  president jair bolsonaro himself  brazil bolsonaro has said he will not  set aside a single centimeter of  indigenous reservations  since his inauguration several attempts  have been made to limit our rights to  claim our territories  among them a bill barring recognition of  ancestral lands which were not occupied  by 1988  when brazil's democratic constitution  was approved  indigenous leaders say that fixing a  cut-off date violates their  constitutional rights  the bill doesn't take into account that  many were violently expelled from their  lands  before 1988  we fought for our rights when the  constitution was being drafted we will  fight against those who want to override  our constitutional rights  the protesters were hoping the supreme  court would rule that the bill violates  the constitution  the decision was expected this week  but at the last minute it was postponed  until august  leaving many here frustrated but  undefeated  these indigenous protesters are already  packing their bags to leave brasilia but  they  say this is not their last supper they  will return in august  to fight for their rights yanakiv  al-jazeera brasilia
 

